// recomp_seeds.json — this game's recompiler seeds (external/psxport/tools/recomp/emit.py --seeds).
//
// The recompiler discovers functions from the binary itself: the entry point, the pointer/table scans,
// then the direct-`jal` call graph. What it CANNOT see is anything reached only through a function
// pointer, or entered at a runtime-computed re-entry point, or an overlay's load base. Those are facts
// about THIS executable and THIS disc, so the framework ships none of them and every game supplies its
// own file. JSON with `//` comments; every key is optional.
//
// RE-03 now proves the overlay map. RE-02's executable seed lists remain empty because they grow from
// real runtime dispatch misses, but the recompiler can place all 22 known code modules without a
// guessed base.
//
// Two rules, both learned the hard way elsewhere in this workspace:
//
//   * NEVER copy another game's seed list. A foreign seed that lands inside your text SPLITS a real
//     function at an arbitrary offset — the emit SUCCEEDS and the recomp is silently corrupt. And note
//     that unlike the sibling ports there is no decomp of this game to lift a seed out of even if the
//     rule allowed it: every address here must come from reproducible binary evidence on SLUS_008.93
//     or from a `[recomp-MISS] 0x800xxxxx` fail-fast on a booting port.
//   * NEVER guess an overlay base. An overlay is keyed BY its load address, so a wrong base emits a
//     whole module of correctly-decoded instructions at wrong addresses, and every jal target, pointer
//     test and router lookup is then silently wrong. A missing base is a HARD ERROR in emit.py,
//     deliberately — that refusal is the feature.
//
// THIS GAME HAS 22 PROVEN CODE MODULES: LEVEL01..LEVEL10/{LEVEL,LEVEL1}.BIN (20), BITS/MEMORY.BIN,
// and FMV/FMV.BIN. The original plain-overlay census measured 245,148 bytes across the first 21;
// FMV/FMV.BIN is 510,960 bytes and mixes code/data, so its whole size is not folded into that old
// code-byte percentage. LEVEL00/LEVEL.BIN is a 4-byte placeholder holding no code. The earlier
// 0x800D1000 result was a 4 KiB-granular FIT, not a load address. RE-03 follows the actual callers:
// every LEVEL filename goes through one wrapper that passes 0x800D12C0; BITS/MEMORY.BIN and
// FMV/FMV.BIN are mutually exclusive contents loaded at 0x800D5D20. The FMV loader at 0x8003EEAC is
// followed immediately by a call to 0x800D6628, offset 0x908 inside those freshly loaded retail
// bytes. A live boot independently reached that exact target after the ESRB card. The 19,040-byte gap
// between the LEVEL and MEMORY/FMV slots equals the largest LEVEL module, and five LEVEL+LEVEL1 pairs
// exceed it, proving the four level names select alternative contents of ONE slot.
// `python3 tools/overlay_map.py --check` re-derives and gates this file and the physical slot map.
//
// Grow the main list empirically: boot, and when the substrate fail-fasts with `[recomp-MISS]
// 0x800xxxxx`, add that address WITH a note on how it is reached. An address with no rationale is
// unreviewable later.
{
  "main": [],
  "main_reentry": [
    // Default live boot, 2026-08-21: the IRQ poll called the guest pointer stored at 0x8009ECD0
    // on the path that later reaches CD command/completion poll 0x80091DE4, producing [recomp-MISS]
    // 0x80088A2C with sp=0x8009FCB0. This is a MID-FUNCTION re-entry, not a function root:
    // the first instruction branches on live v0, there is no prologue, and the path ends by
    // restoring ra/s0 and adding 0x18 to sp at 0x80088A8C..0x80088A98. Ghidra auto-analysis found
    // no containing function and only created FUN_80088A2C when asked, corroborating that ordinary
    // direct-call discovery cannot see the runtime resume target. psxport 692b9b20 makes
    // main_reentry an authoritative discovery root and emits its wrapper/body/dispatch directly;
    // duplicating it in main would create two apparent authorities for one measured entry class.
    "0x80088A2C"
  ],
  "overlay_bases": {
    "BITS__MEMORY": "0x800D5D20",
    "FMV__FMV": "0x800D5D20"
  },
  "overlay_base_patterns": [
    ["LEVEL(?:0[1-9]|10)__LEVEL[0-3]?", "0x800D12C0"]
  ],
  "overlay_seeds": {
    // FUN_8003EE4C loads fmv\\fmv.bin at 0x800D5D20, then its jal at 0x8003EEC4 enters
    // 0x800D6628. The live port reached the same target with FMV bytes resident after present 900.
    // This computed overlay entry is invisible to the emitter's per-module direct-call scan.
    "FMV__FMV": ["0x800D6628"]
  }
}
